{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1944 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "1ee5c9467835615fe320f5203e9a838160f33403d68291ac92ccb3ba51a67c5d" :edb/db-before-t 1943 :edb/db-after-t 1944 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535256 50 #inst "2026-10-08T20:34:12.406Z" 13194139535256 true] [17592186092466 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535256 true] [17592186092466 1100 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-2,-3),(-3,2),(2,4).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-3,2)$\n- $(4,-2)$\n- $(-4,2)$" 13194139535256 true] [17592186092466 1100 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-3), (-3, 2), (2, 4)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S^{′}$, the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation $R_{-90^{∘}}$ by an angle of $90^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n1. $(-3, 2)$\n2. $(4,-2)$\n3. $(-4, 2)$" 13194139535256 false] [17592186092466 1145 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${270}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-3,2),(2,3),(4,-2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535256 true] [17592186092467 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535256 true] [17592186092467 1100 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-1), (1, 4), (1,-1)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S^{′}$, the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation $R_{π/2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{π}{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n1. $(4,-2)$\n2. $(1,-2)$\n3. $(1, 1)$" 13194139535256 false] [17592186092467 1100 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-1),(1,4),(1,-1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(4,-2)$\n- $(1,-2)$\n- $(1,1)$" 13194139535256 true] [17592186092467 1145 "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,-2),(-4,1),(1,1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535256 true] [17592186283725 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/assets/0c2f0240b962701c2eb2ee34a0eddc164afb8e1a677a5ed7f81fe65efb890358.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535256 true] [17592186283725 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/0c2f0240b962701c2eb2ee34a0eddc164afb8e1a677a5ed7f81fe65efb890358.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535256 false] [17592186283725 1145 "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/assets/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/assets/578b3315028bf5a19a4b6e17d228db08b41f24dee63e5cb7f8d601ce667785f4.png)" 13194139535256 true] [17592186283725 1145 "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/578b3315028bf5a19a4b6e17d228db08b41f24dee63e5cb7f8d601ce667785f4.png)" 13194139535256 false] [17592186286130 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059586/assets/f7f75505591eb8bf407e63b4255ae65548bde65921f6f00cca9031191ddc0905.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535256 true] [17592186286130 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003089/assets/f7f75505591eb8bf407e63b4255ae65548bde65921f6f00cca9031191ddc0905.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535256 false] [17592186286130 1145 "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\